[1] In 1896, visitors to Coney Island in New York could ride a strange new attraction: a moving ramp that carried passengers up a gentle slope at an angle of about 25 degrees. [2] Its inventor, Jesse Reno, called it an "inclined elevator." [3] {{1|Riders stood on a belt of wooden slats while a motor pulled the belt along, and the ride lasted only a few seconds.}}

[4] Within a few years, {{2|other inventors, were}} working on their own versions. [5] Charles Seeberger designed a moving stairway with flat steps that rose from a level platform, and he named it an "escalator," combining the Latin word for steps with the word "elevator." [6] The elevator company Otis bought Seeberger's patents and displayed its machine at the 1900 Paris Exposition, where it won a prize.

[7] Department stores were among the first buyers. [8] Store owners had noticed that shoppers rarely climbed stairs to reach upper floors {{3|and that the elevators, which held only a few people at a time, often had long lines.}} [9] An escalator could carry hundreds of people an hour and {{4|doesn't require}} an operator. [10] {{5|However,}} because riders could see the merchandise on each floor as they rose, the moving stairs encouraged them to browse.

[11] For decades, Otis treated "escalator" as a brand name and used it in {{6|their}} advertising. [12] Other companies had to call their machines "moving stairways." [13] In 1950, however, a federal office ruled that the word had become so common in everyday speech that it no longer identified a single company's product. [14] {{7|After the ruling, any manufacturer could use the word.}}

[15] Today, escalators carry millions of people each day through subway stations, airports, and shopping centers. [16] Few riders know that the word beneath their feet once {{8|belonged to}} a single company. [17] {{9|}}
